{
  "$schema": "./schema.json",
  "day": 32,
  "level": 2,
  "title": { "en": "Body and feelings", "es": "El cuerpo" },
  "objectives": [
    "Name the parts of the body you'd mention at a pharmacy",
    "Say what hurts with me duele and me duelen",
    "Say you're tired or ill with estoy cansado and estoy enfermo"
  ],
  "teach": [
    {
      "kind": "note",
      "md": "**The body**\n\n- **la cabeza**: head\n- **la garganta**: throat\n- **el estómago**: stomach\n- **la espalda**: back\n- **la mano**: hand. It ends in -o but is **feminine**: *la mano*.\n- **el pie**: foot\n\n*el cuerpo* = the body."
    },
    {
      "kind": "note",
      "md": "**me duele works like me gusta.** The thing that hurts is the subject:\n\n- one thing → **me duele**: *Me duele la cabeza.* (I have a headache.)\n- several → **me duelen**: *Me duelen los pies.*\n\nUse **la, el, los**, not *my*: *me duele **la** cabeza*, never ~~me duele mi cabeza~~."
    },
    { "kind": "phrase", "es": "Me duele la garganta.", "en": "I have a sore throat.", "note": "Madrid's dry winter air makes this a classic. At the farmacia, the pharmacist can help with minor things without a prescription." },
    {
      "kind": "note",
      "md": "**estar for how you are.** On day 23, *estar* said where you are. It also says how you are **right now**:\n\n- **Estoy cansado** (a man) / **cansada** (a woman): I'm tired.\n- **Estoy enfermo / enferma**: I'm ill.\n\nThe adjective agrees with the speaker, like nationalities and colours."
    },
    { "kind": "phrase", "es": "Estoy muy cansada. Me duelen los pies.", "en": "I'm very tired. My feet hurt.", "note": "After a day of walking from the Prado to the Palacio Real." },
    { "kind": "phrase", "es": "Estoy enfermo. Necesito ir a la farmacia.", "en": "I'm ill. I need to go to the pharmacy.", "note": "In an emergency anywhere in Spain or the EU, call 112." }
  ],
  "vocab": [
